---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/style.css";

// O.K. — work page for edsonpavoni.art
// v1.0 SAFE (2026-09-16): the short, publishable version. English only.
// The fuller draft (video slots, the name audio, making of, quote bank) is parked at
// 0000 AI/artworks/ok-shamel-pitts/website/ok-FULL-DRAFT-PARKED-2026-09-16.astro
//
// Rules for this page:
//   - Shamel first in every credit. "Tushrik Fredericks". Official credits: SCRIPTS-3-VIDEOS.md:30.
//   - Nothing from the private recordings (Look Inwards, the name audio) until Shamel says yes.
//   - Mechanism copy follows the deck ("transmitted in real time"), not "every single beat".

const base = "/assets/pages/ok";

// LOOPS — muted autoplay videos made from Edson's GIFs (GIF → webm + mp4 + poster).
// To add one: convert into public/assets/pages/ok/, then add a line here.
const loops = [
    { name: "loop", caption: "The paper body of the sculpture, moving." },
];
---

<Layout title="O.K. - Edson Pavoni">
    <main>
        <section>
            <div class="container">
                <div class="welcome-tile margin-top-900">
                    <span class="fs-logo margin-top-600">Edson Pavoni</span>
                    <h1 class="fs-display fw-bold text-positive margin-top-200">
                        O.K.
                    </h1>
                </div>

                <div class="fs-label fstyle-label margin-top-600">
                    kinetic sculpture, dance and film / 2026
                </div>

                <div class="artwork | margin-top-500">
                    <span class="fs-sub-title">Can a machine earn its place in dance?</span><br /><br />
                    An artwork by Shamel Pitts | TRIBE and Edson Pavoni.
                    <br /><br />
                    Two dancers face each other. From beginning to end, they remain in search of eye contact.
                    Each wears a heart rate sensor against the skin.
                    <br /><br />
                    Between them stands a kinetic sculpture made of folded paper, with two sides that move
                    independently. One side answers to Shamel's heart, the other to Tushrik's. As their
                    heart rates rise, the sculpture moves faster. As they calm, its motion slows.
                    <br /><br />
                    The human body leads, and the machine doesn't follow: it witnesses, holding space for
                    the ritual between them.
                </div>

                <img
                    class="margin-top-300"
                    src={`${base}/ok_03.jpg`}
                    width="2048"
                    height="1152"
                    alt="Shamel Pitts and Tushrik Fredericks facing each other across the stage, the folded paper sculpture between them."
                />
                <div class="fs-caption text-caption padding-block-caption">
                    <span class="fstyle-label">O.K.</span>, film still.
                </div>

                <div class="margin-top-500">
                    <span class="fs-sub-title">One gesture</span>
                    <br /><br />
                    The choreography draws from the stare-down before a boxing match. Just before the fight,
                    the two opponents stand face to face, not to fight, but to recognize each other as mythic
                    doubles. In O.K. the stare-down becomes a stare inward: the ritual of seeing ourselves in
                    the other, raw, distorted and divine.
                    <br /><br />
                    The dancers move in relation to each other without ever touching. Their movements shift
                    from stillness to intensity, and the sculpture echoes the arc.
                </div>

                <!-- cut2: documentation/assets/cut2.mov (also exported as gif_cut2.gif) -->
                <video data-autoplay preload="none" muted playsinline loop poster={`${base}/cut2_poster.jpg`} class="video-scale margin-top-400">
                    <source src={`${base}/cut2.webm`} type="video/webm" />
                    <source src={`${base}/cut2.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
                </video>
                <div class="fs-caption text-caption padding-block-caption">
                    Hand to pulse. The gesture that starts the sculpture.
                </div>

                <div class="margin-top-400">
                    When the dancers meet there is an invitation. An "OK" that signals the ignition of the
                    dance, a mutual consent to begin. At the end, after the tension and the release, one
                    dancer walks away, and his side of the sculpture goes still. The dancer who remains turns
                    to the camera and asks, simply: OK?
                    <br /><br />
                    A final invitation, for the viewer to listen to their own heartbeat, and to begin their
                    own dance.
                </div>

                <div class="fs-eye | margin-top-400">
                    Man and machine allowing themselves to soften in the context of pressure.
                </div>

                <img class="margin-top-400" src={`${base}/ok_02.jpg`}
                    width="2048"
                    height="1152" loading="lazy" alt="The two dancers in a wide duet in front of the paper sculpture." />
                <img class="margin-top-200" src={`${base}/ok_06.jpg`}
                    width="2048"
                    height="1152" loading="lazy" alt="The two dancers in an embrace, close to the folded paper surface." />
                <img class="margin-top-200" src={`${base}/ok_04.jpg`}
                    width="2048"
                    height="1152" loading="lazy" alt="Shamel Pitts alone, bent over, breathing, in front of the sculpture." />
                <div class="fs-caption text-caption padding-block-caption">
                    Stills from <span class="fstyle-label">O.K.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="margin-top-600">
                    <span class="fs-sub-title">The techno heart</span>
                    <br /><br />
                    Shamel nicknamed the sculpture the techno heart. It was made with paper, mechanics,
                    electronics and software, all built for this piece.
                    <br /><br />
                    Each dancer's heartbeat is transmitted in real time from a sensor on the body to a
                    computer, which drives two linear actuators, one for each side of the sculpture. Two
                    heart rhythms shaping one physical form.
                </div>

                {loops.map((l) => (
                    <>
                        <video data-autoplay preload="none"
                            muted
                            playsinline
                            loop
                            poster={`${base}/${l.name}_poster.jpg`}
                            class="video-scale margin-top-300"
                        >
                            <source src={`${base}/${l.name}.webm`} type="video/webm" />
                            <source src={`${base}/${l.name}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
                        </video>
                        <div class="fs-caption text-caption padding-block-caption">{l.caption}</div>
                    </>
                ))}

                <div class="margin-top-400">
                    The creature's body is paper, folded by hand using traditional origami tessellations.
                    The folds act like a score: they decide how it can move before it ever moves.
                    <br /><br />
                    Inside, the mechanics are inspired by the muscle of the human heart, contracting and
                    releasing to give the creature its movement vocabulary.
                    <br /><br />
                    The electronics and code were custom built, and they never ask for attention. The bodies
                    in motion are the protagonists.
                </div>

                <div class="fs-eye | margin-top-400">
                    A witnessing machine that holds two hearts in one body.
                </div>

                <div class="margin-top-600">
                    <span class="fs-sub-title">A research into the poetics of technology in dance</span>
                    <br /><br />
                    The work began with motion tracking, pose estimation and biomechanical skeleton models,
                    filmed at high frame rate, to see how dance defies prediction. After that exploration,
                    the research moved in a different direction: toward the physicality of sculpture, and
                    away from the pixel as output.
                    <br /><br />
                    No screen. No music. The only sound is the breath, the steps, and the machine.
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fstyle-label">O.K.</span> is an attempt at a new ritual, composed of the
                    sound of every breath, every step, an intimate camera, sometimes extremely close, and a
                    machine that holds two hearts. With TRIBE, the research continues.
                </div>

                <!-- cut1: documentation/assets/cut1.mov (also exported as gif_cut1.gif). Moved near the end, Edson 2026-09-16. -->
                <video data-autoplay preload="none" muted playsinline loop poster={`${base}/cut1_poster.jpg`} class="video-scale margin-top-400">
                    <source src={`${base}/cut1.webm`} type="video/webm" />
                    <source src={`${base}/cut1.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
                </video>
                <div class="fs-caption text-caption padding-block-caption">
                    The duet, and the sculpture moving with it.
                </div>

                <!-- CREDITS — official list, SCRIPTS-3-VIDEOS.md:30. Do not reorder. -->
                <div class="fs-credits | margin-top-500">
                    <span class="fs-sub-title">Credits</span>
                    <br /><br />
                    Commissioned by Jacob's Pillow with support from YoungArts, produced by TRIBE.
                    Developed at Onassis ONX Studio, New York.
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fs-credits-bold">Artists</span><br />
                    Shamel Pitts, Tushrik Fredericks and Edson Pavoni
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fs-credits-bold">Curator</span><br />
                    Pamela Tatge
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fs-credits-bold">Dance</span><br />
                    Choreography: Shamel Pitts | TRIBE<br />
                    Dancers: Shamel Pitts and Tushrik Fredericks
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fs-credits-bold">Film</span><br />
                    Director: Edson Pavoni<br />
                    Assistant Director: Arjun Hassard<br />
                    Director of Photography: Jamie Ellington<br />
                    Editing, Audio and Color: Edson Pavoni
                    <br /><br />
                    <span class="fs-credits-bold">Special thanks</span><br />
                    Jazia Hammoudi, Aaron Santiago, Matthew Niederhauser and Matt Toups, from the Onassis ONX team.<br />
                    David Imani, Jared Fine, Nick Kepley and Bonnie Spann, from Jacob's Pillow.<br />
                    Brittany Wilson, E Katrina Lewis, Eric Umble and Mirelle Martins, from TRIBE.
                    <br /><br /><br /><br />
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
